<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2024-12-29.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="000551"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000551" label="[00:05:51]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">here's my patch to jamvm t get it to compile <a rel="nofollow" href="https://mega.nz/file/pgxEmZrA#bymW48EBdC5wME-pVERyz21ODTs-7aJXodB4rWcX_Yo">https://mega.nz/file/pgxEmZrA#bymW48EBdC5wME-pVERyz21ODTs-7aJXodB4rWcX_Yo</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="000624"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000624" label="[00:06:24]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but apparently i compiled without support for -Xbootclasspath which is kinda silly</span></div><div class="line" id="000649"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000649" label="[00:06:49]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so, again, whatever configure options would be handy on the bootstrapping page</span></div><div class="line" id="000826"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000826" label="[00:08:26]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'm guessing --with-classpath-install-dir</span></div><div class="line" id="001244"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001244" label="[00:12:44]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and i guess i compiled without classpath being in its default location hrm</span></div><div class="line" id="025622"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#025622" label="[02:56:22]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: actually we looked into asic costs and we could do a custom asic (without pin restrictions) for as little as $9.75K or if we are willing to have pin restrictions: $500 <a rel="nofollow" href="https://tinytapeout.com/teaching/">https://tinytapeout.com/teaching/</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="025819"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#025819" label="[02:58:19]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">So us doing custom asic work is definitely something we could afford if someone is willing to do the work on designing custom hardware to help with bootstrapping.</span></div><div class="line" id="025858"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#025858" label="[02:58:58]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: thank you for the link, didn't know anyone took the time to get that working in guix</span></div><div class="line" id="030807"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#030807" label="[03:08:07]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(skywater fab being 130nm which is sufficient for 64bit multicore CPUs with 1MB of L2)</span></div><div class="line" id="030905"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#030905" label="[03:09:05]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">not to mention we can leverage other people's work: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://bitlog.it/20220118_asic_roundup_of_open_source_riscv_cpu_cores.html">https://bitlog.it/20220118_asic_roundup_of_open_source_riscv_cpu_cores.html</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="030908"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#030908" label="[03:09:08]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">what's wrong with RISCV64?</span></div><div class="line" id="031220"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#031220" label="[03:12:20]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: nothing; (I just didn't get to a link of 64bit RISC-V chips yet)</span></div><div class="line" id="031330"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#031330" label="[03:13:30]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the point being, we are not limited to what is readily available and we can do custom hardware to make bootstrapping easier (if we have hardware people interested in exploring that solution space)</span></div><div class="line" id="031356"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#031356" label="[03:13:56]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">just for comparison, most of my bare metal tests have been on a 65nm quad-core, so 130nm is about 1/4 density</span></div><div class="line" id="031650"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#031650" label="[03:16:50]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&amp; of course, with custom hardware, it can be designed in such a way to facilitate truly bare metal bootstrapping - e.g. with enough memory available right from power-on to get TCC bootstrapped, so that any memory training (if needed at all) can be done using C code</span></div><div class="line" id="031736"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#031736" label="[03:17:36]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">plus readily available UARTs / SPI / etc. available via MMIO without any tricky initialization</span></div><div class="line" id="031850"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#031850" label="[03:18:50]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(and writing the firmware/bios is much easier of a problem for hardware we design)</span></div><div class="line" id="031931"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#031931" label="[03:19:31]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ideally, the firmwre is part of what we bootstrap :)</span></div><div class="line" id="032012"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#032012" label="[03:20:12]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I wonder if there is a simple, free IP core out there implementing basic x86-64-v1</span></div><div class="line" id="032059"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#032059" label="[03:20:59]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">not that I have seen found yet; plus we don't need *FULL* AMD64 support only the subset used by us</span></div><div class="line" id="032126"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#032126" label="[03:21:26]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">should be possible given the age of the architecture - more than 20 years passed since the first implementation came out from AMD</span></div><div class="line" id="032154"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#032154" label="[03:21:54]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so any patents should be expired - again, I'm only talking about the basics (what GCC calls &quot;v1&quot;)</span></div><div class="line" id="032717"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#032717" label="[03:27:17]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">unfortunately this seems to be the most capable free x86 core out there: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/alfikpl/ao486">https://github.com/alfikpl/ao486</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="033000"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#033000" label="[03:30:00]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'm interested in x86 mainly because of Haskell - I simply don't see any other architecture ever successfully bootstrap GHC without cooperation from GHC's developers, which seems to be sorely missing</span></div><div class="line" id="034435"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#034435" label="[03:44:35]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/vhdlf/cpu_basic">https://github.com/vhdlf/cpu_basic</a>  - looks like x86, described as &quot;a pentium-like 32-bit CPU&quot; and &quot;It follows the instruction format of Intel x86 processors&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="035229"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#035229" label="[03:52:29]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">what are those unregistered HC files of ghc 4.08?</span></div><div class="line" id="035418"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#035418" label="[03:54:18]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Guix contains a comment saying that they cause segfaults</span></div><div class="line" id="035454"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#035454" label="[03:54:54]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">But even if they do build (yielding an extremely slow GHC), it's gonna cause problems in later versions</span></div><div class="line" id="035504"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#035504" label="[03:55:04]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">AFAIK unregisterised builds are dead in newer GHC</span></div><div class="line" id="035723"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#035723" label="[03:57:23]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and also arm support is pretty recent, so no way for arm boostrap</span></div><div class="line" id="035730"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#035730" label="[03:57:30]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">riscv is in worse situation</span></div><div class="line" id="035842"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#035842" label="[03:58:42]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'm not even sure if riscv is supported in the most recent GHC</span></div><div class="line" id="035852"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#035852" label="[03:58:52]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it is not</span></div><div class="line" id="035916"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#035916" label="[03:59:16]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I wonder how we're even able to desktops on riscv then...</span></div><div class="line" id="035939"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#035939" label="[03:59:39]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">given that modern GUI frameworks all seem to depend on Haskell in one way or another</span></div><div class="line" id="035949"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#035949" label="[03:59:49]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">*to have desktios</span></div><div class="line" id="035950"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#035950" label="[03:59:50]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">they don't</span></div><div class="line" id="035953"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#035953" label="[03:59:53]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's what i keep saying over and over</span></div><div class="line" id="040002"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#040002" label="[04:00:02]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">haskell is important for pandoc but it's very, very easy to have a haskell-free system</span></div><div class="line" id="040010"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#040010" label="[04:00:10]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas said the same the last time this came up</span></div><div class="line" id="040016"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#040016" label="[04:00:16]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">most gentoo systems don't have it</span></div><div class="line" id="040032"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#040032" label="[04:00:32]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and gnome depends on rust</span></div><div class="line" id="040046"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#040046" label="[04:00:46]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">sure, rust isn't the same as haskell, rust is a different situation</span></div><div class="line" id="040048"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#040048" label="[04:00:48]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we do have mrustc there...</span></div><div class="line" id="040103"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#040103" label="[04:01:03]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and a guarantee that rustc will never depend on its own quirks</span></div><div class="line" id="040119"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#040119" label="[04:01:19]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but that doesn't help on 32-bit</span></div><div class="line" id="040129"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#040129" label="[04:01:29]">&lt;unmush`&gt;</a></span><span class="message">last I checked rust doesn't have a standard, so isn't *all* rust behavior a rustc quirk?</span></div><div class="line" id="040423"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#040423" label="[04:04:23]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'm referring to the rule that rustc version N, compiled in release mode (which disables all undocumented, experimental and in-development features) must be able to compile rustc version N+1</span></div><div class="line" id="040440"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#040440" label="[04:04:40]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(I'm not sure that's even a rule, fwiw, at least in the past I've not been able to find docs for that)</span></div><div class="line" id="040444"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#040444" label="[04:04:44]">&lt;unmush`&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ah, I see</span></div><div class="line" id="040455"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#040455" label="[04:04:55]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">as far as I'm aware it's an unofficial rule of thumb for what may work</span></div><div class="line" id="040506"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#040506" label="[04:05:06]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">AFAIK that's how they generate the official binaries</span></div><div class="line" id="040541"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#040541" label="[04:05:41]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I don't know of any modern gui frameworks that use haskell</span></div><div class="line" id="040543"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#040543" label="[04:05:43]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it also certainly isn't true for all targets anyway, because i've filed bugs when it didn't work before, and the response was super unhelpful</span></div><div class="line" id="040548"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#040548" label="[04:05:48]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and I don't see them packaged in guix</span></div><div class="line" id="040551"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#040551" label="[04:05:51]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and i had to figure out which patch fixed it by myself</span></div><div class="line" id="040630"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#040630" label="[04:06:30]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/b76036c88c1fa7b63f412e777e185336848c52d6/src/tools/rust-installer/make-tarballs.sh#L14">https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/b76036c88c1fa7b63f412e777e185336848c52d6/src/tools/rust-installer/make-tarballs.sh#L14</a>  btw</span></div><div class="line" id="040639"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#040639" label="[04:06:39]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so yeah, no such guarantee AFAIK in both senses</span></div><div class="line" id="040644"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#040644" label="[04:06:44]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(N/N-1, and also release vs nightly)</span></div><div class="line" id="040700"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#040700" label="[04:07:00]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">on the other hand, rust is infecting everything</span></div><div class="line" id="040819"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#040819" label="[04:08:19]">&lt;unmush`&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I would love to go around countermeming with &quot;rewrite it in ADA!&quot;, but ada somehow is even worse for bootstrapping</span></div><div class="line" id="040821"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#040821" label="[04:08:21]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: xmonad uses haskell (but perhaps you might not consider that a modern gui framework?)</span></div><div class="line" id="040847"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#040847" label="[04:08:47]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ada at least has a specification even if it is terrible for bootstrapping too</span></div><div class="line" id="040853"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#040853" label="[04:08:53]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and actually has multiple production compilers (even if only one is FOSS)</span></div><div class="line" id="040858"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#040858" label="[04:08:58]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">xmonad is a window manager, not gui framework</span></div><div class="line" id="040934"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#040934" label="[04:09:34]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">also cute that ada's spec is freely available (no hacks needed like for C, C++, and so on where you share drafts)</span></div><div class="line" id="041017"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#041017" label="[04:10:17]">&lt;unmush`&gt;</a></span><span class="message">one of the few times it was an unambiguously good thing US taxpayers funded something</span></div><div class="line" id="041026"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#041026" label="[04:10:26]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">:)</span></div><div class="line" id="041039"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#041039" label="[04:10:39]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and yes, xmonad missed on wayland, so is not modern</span></div><div class="line" id="041046"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#041046" label="[04:10:46]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">home: fair (and this list shows none are: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_platform-independent_GUI_libraries">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_platform-independent_GUI_libraries</a>  )</span></div><div class="line" id="041053"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#041053" label="[04:10:53]">&lt;unmush`&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if only all research and development grants resulted in such open results</span></div><div class="line" id="041158"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#041158" label="[04:11:58]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I believe it's mostly indirect dependencies like the aforementioned pandoc</span></div><div class="line" id="041214"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#041214" label="[04:12:14]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">even then, entirely very much avoidable</span></div><div class="line" id="041216"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#041216" label="[04:12:16]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which is unfortunately required for everything, at least in Guix</span></div><div class="line" id="041219"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#041219" label="[04:12:19]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's nowhere near as critical as rust</span></div><div class="line" id="041247"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#041247" label="[04:12:47]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well the biggest problem with Ada bootstrapping is just how much of the language one would need to support to build any of the Ada compilers written in Ada (which is more effort than the year of work in the Ada Compiler we can bootstrap)</span></div><div class="line" id="041250"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#041250" label="[04:12:50]">&lt;unmush`&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hoping that that weight-watchers team goes somewhere</span></div><div class="line" id="041254"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#041254" label="[04:12:54]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">How is pandoc avoidable?</span></div><div class="line" id="041259"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#041259" label="[04:12:59]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">.. disable building man pages?</span></div><div class="line" id="041318"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#041318" label="[04:13:18]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">You can't</span></div><div class="line" id="041322"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#041322" label="[04:13:22]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Maybe in Gentoo, you can</span></div><div class="line" id="041324"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#041324" label="[04:13:24]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">But not in Guix</span></div><div class="line" id="041326"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#041326" label="[04:13:26]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well yeah that's my point</span></div><div class="line" id="041333"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#041333" label="[04:13:33]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's a guix thing, not a fundamental desktop ecosystem thing</span></div><div class="line" id="041334"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#041334" label="[04:13:34]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">not easily in guix but possible if you do your own tree</span></div><div class="line" id="041348"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#041348" label="[04:13:48]">&lt;unmush`&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you can, you just have to accept the call on the long night of local builds</span></div><div class="line" id="041354"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#041354" label="[04:13:54]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which means it doesn't make sense to say &quot;how can we have desktops on riscv w/o haskell&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="041415"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#041415" label="[04:14:15]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but honestly even then, not that much even uses pandoc given this</span></div><div class="line" id="041421"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#041421" label="[04:14:21]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(even if you always build the man pages)</span></div><div class="line" id="041450"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#041450" label="[04:14:50]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">haskell is really so insignificant in bootstrapping importance compared to rust</span></div><div class="line" id="041500"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#041500" label="[04:15:00]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">rust is borderline unavoidable for many usecases now</span></div><div class="line" id="041507"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#041507" label="[04:15:07]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">The problem is, if X depends on Y which depends on Z which depends on... which depends on Pandoc, then Haskell is a transitive dependency of X</span></div><div class="line" id="041526"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#041526" label="[04:15:26]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i fully understand, i'm saying: a) not much depends on it to begin with, and b) the issue you started with is guix-specific anyway</span></div><div class="line" id="041626"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#041626" label="[04:16:26]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Unfortunately the only other option that is buildable in a reproducible way is Nix, which has the exact same issue</span></div><div class="line" id="041636"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#041636" label="[04:16:36]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: or if you can get guix to finally merge: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://blog.lispy.tech/parameterized-packages-the-project-completion-update.html">https://blog.lispy.tech/parameterized-packages-the-project-completion-update.html</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="041645"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#041645" label="[04:16:45]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: i had assumed guix had that technology now</span></div><div class="line" id="041706"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#041706" label="[04:17:06]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">also, doesn't nix have build options? I was sure it did</span></div><div class="line" id="041712"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#041712" label="[04:17:12]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(even if guix doesn't)</span></div><div class="line" id="041731"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#041731" label="[04:17:31]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">AFAIK it lacks the ability to make dependencies conditional</span></div><div class="line" id="041735"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#041735" label="[04:17:35]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ah</span></div><div class="line" id="041743"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#041743" label="[04:17:43]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so maybe can toggle build configuration but not the deps themselves or something</span></div><div class="line" id="041803"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#041803" label="[04:18:03]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Guix and Nix both lack a notion of a package being e.g. &quot;a dependency for running unit tests&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="041814"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#041814" label="[04:18:14]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">or &quot;a dependency for building documentation&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="041902"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#041902" label="[04:19:02]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but if someone just made that metadata available it could</span></div><div class="line" id="041923"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#041923" label="[04:19:23]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and could probably simplify the build descriptions too</span></div><div class="line" id="041950"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#041950" label="[04:19:50]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">as one could build a package's documentation and install it but not build the package itself</span></div><div class="line" id="042000"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#042000" label="[04:20:00]">&lt;unmush`&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think it would require some ergonomic way of specifying attributes for edges in the dependency graph</span></div><div class="line" id="042017"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#042017" label="[04:20:17]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I myself actually get shocked how much gets built, like to build pipewire, you have to build gstreamer, qt5, qt6 and gtk3</span></div><div class="line" id="042034"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#042034" label="[04:20:34]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and a lot of other elephant dependencies</span></div><div class="line" id="042042"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#042042" label="[04:20:42]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i find this kind of baffling because this unnecessarily makes the depgraph huge for small installations</span></div><div class="line" id="042052"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#042052" label="[04:20:52]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">very surprised to learn this</span></div><div class="line" id="042112"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#042112" label="[04:21:12]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i bet you have to have webkit-gtk for emacs too</span></div><div class="line" id="042117"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#042117" label="[04:21:17]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">The only distinction that's made is build-time vs runtime dependencies</span></div><div class="line" id="042130"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#042130" label="[04:21:30]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: you can avoid pipewire for a bit but some packages hardcode it in</span></div><div class="line" id="042139"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#042139" label="[04:21:39]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">And gstreamer and stuff are optional for pipewire</span></div><div class="line" id="042154"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#042154" label="[04:21:54]">&lt;unmush`&gt;</a></span><span class="message">guix graph: error: no path from 'emacs@29.4' to 'webkitgtk@2.44.1'</span></div><div class="line" id="042201"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#042201" label="[04:22:01]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">a lot of optional stuff is enabled because why not</span></div><div class="line" id="042216"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#042216" label="[04:22:16]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">also you can't install both ffmpeg and mpv in your packages (because mpv pulls in ffmpeg too and they are conflicting versions)</span></div><div class="line" id="042222"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#042222" label="[04:22:22]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">wait what</span></div><div class="line" id="042225"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#042225" label="[04:22:25]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">what what what</span></div><div class="line" id="042229"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#042229" label="[04:22:29]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">because that's obviously an issue we hit in gentoo</span></div><div class="line" id="042234"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#042234" label="[04:22:34]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but like, isn't the whole thing with nix and guix that you can avoid that</span></div><div class="line" id="042238"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#042238" label="[04:22:38]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">because package installs are isolaed?</span></div><div class="line" id="042239"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#042239" label="[04:22:39]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">*isolated</span></div><div class="line" id="042247"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#042247" label="[04:22:47]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">webkitgtk and rust are the reason I will move away from gnome, I cannot easily rebuild those</span></div><div class="line" id="042252"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#042252" label="[04:22:52]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Pandoc is usually a build-time dependency, so you can install packages depending on it from substitutes without also pulling in a binary of Pandoc - but if you're building, you do need it</span></div><div class="line" id="042300"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#042300" label="[04:23:00]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">*on guix*, yes</span></div><div class="line" id="042311"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#042311" label="[04:23:11]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but it's a guix-specific issue, not some sort of deep ecosystem problem</span></div><div class="line" id="042311"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#042311" label="[04:23:11]">&lt;unmush`&gt;</a></span><span class="message">sam_: allow me to introduce you to a despised guix hack: propagated-inputs</span></div><div class="line" id="042347"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#042347" label="[04:23:47]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">unmush`: wrt webkit-gtk: it was/is for 'xwidgets' in emacs, but it looks like we ripped it out in gentoo in September because it broke w/ newer webkit-gtk (<a rel="nofollow" href="https://debbugs.gnu.org/66068#47">https://debbugs.gnu.org/66068#47</a>) </span></div><div class="line" id="042353"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#042353" label="[04:23:53]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(just explaining that it wasn't a random example)</span></div><div class="line" id="042408"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#042408" label="[04:24:08]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">test depends are also a huge problem during build</span></div><div class="line" id="042418"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#042418" label="[04:24:18]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">test dependencies can often be bad, yeah</span></div><div class="line" id="042749"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#042749" label="[04:27:49]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, guix and nix have the idea which is the dream but the details are where things go sideways, it is almost like work saving was opted over better build control. (which honestly makes sense for projects that started out as personal projects)</span></div><div class="line" id="042813"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#042813" label="[04:28:13]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ACTION nods</span></div><div class="line" id="042834"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#042834" label="[04:28:34]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and of course we are not perfect either, i don't want to moan every time someone mentions guix or anything, i just don't like to see the problem presented as a general one</span></div><div class="line" id="042847"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#042847" label="[04:28:47]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">haskell is super avoidable in general and you can have desktops without it trivially</span></div><div class="line" id="042852"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#042852" label="[04:28:52]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">do i want a bootstrappable ghc? absolutely</span></div><div class="line" id="042913"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#042913" label="[04:29:13]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">just, it'll take more time than anyone has yet been able to invest.</span></div><div class="line" id="042926"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#042926" label="[04:29:26]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">webkit-gtk is a funny one because meanwhile I gave up on building firefox &amp; chromium due to annoying dependencies and having a new version like every other week</span></div><div class="line" id="043016"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#043016" label="[04:30:16]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">If I could throw $500 into a pot for anyone willing to solve the Haskell bootstrap problem; I would do so. (then maybe someone might have motivation to do that work which WILL suck)</span></div><div class="line" id="043057"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#043057" label="[04:30:57]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lanodan: have you tried telescope (gemini browser)?</span></div><div class="line" id="043144"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#043144" label="[04:31:44]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Nah, I got stuck to netsurf for like a week once though</span></div><div class="line" id="043235"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#043235" label="[04:32:35]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">As for Gentoo, what's missing is reproducibility</span></div><div class="line" id="043252"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#043252" label="[04:32:52]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yep, i don't dispute that</span></div><div class="line" id="043346"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#043346" label="[04:33:46]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I love kiwix files, I just wish the viewers didn't do a full firefox build to let me read a local wikipedia mirror</span></div><div class="line" id="043633"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#043633" label="[04:36:33]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yeah gentoo having at least reproducible stage3 tarballs would be neat, that said part of why I got interested in bootstrapping is stage3 tarballs being like a huge blob that you can't verify</span></div><div class="line" id="043707"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#043707" label="[04:37:07]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes</span></div><div class="line" id="043805"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#043805" label="[04:38:05]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">mid-kid and myself did achieve building a stage3 tarball on top of live-bootstrap without pulling in a prebuilt stage3, but without reproducibility, it's of limited utility</span></div><div class="line" id="043945"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#043945" label="[04:39:45]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: well that is a pretty great utility of trust for a stage3</span></div><div class="line" id="043946"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#043946" label="[04:39:46]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well gentoo is fairly manual and if it can be pretty much repeatable it's fine</span></div><div class="line" id="044035"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#044035" label="[04:40:35]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">my hope there was generating a stage3 with the same hash as the most recent official stage3, thus validating that lineage of stage3 tarballs as not containing a Karger-Thompson attack - but unfortunately even 2 stage3 builds from the same source code on live-bootstrap result in completely different binaries</span></div><div class="line" id="044112"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#044112" label="[04:41:12]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that sounds a bit surprising, the only real difference I'd expect there is some timestamps embedded in the odd application, it's not like we do anything special there</span></div><div class="line" id="044123"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#044123" label="[04:41:23]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">like if you look at <a rel="nofollow" href="https://wiki.gentoo.org/wiki/User:OstCollector/Reproducible_Build">https://wiki.gentoo.org/wiki/User:OstCollector/Reproducible_Build</a>,  most of it is about fixing that in the vdb</span></div><div class="line" id="044127"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#044127" label="[04:41:27]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">not binaries themselves or anything</span></div><div class="line" id="044141"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#044141" label="[04:41:41]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">would really need to see specific examples though</span></div><div class="line" id="044145"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#044145" label="[04:41:45]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but it's doable, just needs someone to do it</span></div><div class="line" id="044239"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#044239" label="[04:42:39]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Guix does solve reproducibility, but you need to pull in bootstrap binaries at the start (because the hashes of those binaries are hardcoded in Guix; a shame especially given the unclear provenance of those bootstrap binaries), plus, you get the whole Haskell issue</span></div><div class="line" id="091631"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#091631" label="[09:16:31]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Reproducing gentoo's stage3 builds is impossible because they're never rebuilt from scratch - they're always updated based on an existing binary cache</span></div><div class="line" id="091748"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#091748" label="[09:17:48]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Portage has no way of knowing what specific package versions were used to build something.</span></div><div class="line" id="091849"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#091849" label="[09:18:49]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's not what he was saying</span></div><div class="line" id="091903"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#091903" label="[09:19:03]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">they're saying that *when starting from live-bootstrap*, building it again gave a different result to when they did earlier</span></div><div class="line" id="091917"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#091917" label="[09:19:17]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we're not talking about &quot;reproducing arbitrary gentoo stage3s&quot; (although that is a nice thing to work towards)</span></div><div class="line" id="091923"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#091923" label="[09:19:23]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(and I suspect we could get there for that, too)</span></div><div class="line" id="091951"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#091951" label="[09:19:51]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i.e. they said they would like to do that bit, but &quot;but unfortunately even 2 stage3 builds from the same source code on live-bootstrap result in completely different binaries&quot; (so &quot;even the easier part&quot; couldn't be done, for some reason)</span></div><div class="line" id="091952"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#091952" label="[09:19:52]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">They specifically mentioned &quot;generating a stage3 with the same hash as the most recent official stage3&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="091955"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#091955" label="[09:19:55]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, I know</span></div><div class="line" id="091959"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#091959" label="[09:19:59]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but they went on to say &quot;but we couldn't even do that&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="092004"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#092004" label="[09:20:04]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">That's what I'm commenting on</span></div><div class="line" id="092006"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#092006" label="[09:20:06]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Hm</span></div><div class="line" id="092012"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#092012" label="[09:20:12]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's not really a meaningful point if you can't reproduce your own ones ;)</span></div><div class="line" id="092027"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#092027" label="[09:20:27]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and if we get to the point where your own ones work, we can talk about making our own releng ones reproducible too</span></div><div class="line" id="092056"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#092056" label="[09:20:56]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I have some initial ideas for making that easier</span></div><div class="line" id="092108"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#092108" label="[09:21:08]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we can talk about that, I just have no idea why the ones built in live-bootstrap themselves were non-reproducible</span></div><div class="line" id="092137"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#092137" label="[09:21:37]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I agree but even if we fix our own ones, the way releng is currently setup there won't be a way to reproduce them</span></div><div class="line" id="092142"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#092142" label="[09:21:42]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">sure</span></div><div class="line" id="092143"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#092143" label="[09:21:43]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I felt that was worth mentioning</span></div><div class="line" id="092158"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#092158" label="[09:21:58]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">just like portage right now needs patches, and so on</span></div><div class="line" id="092200"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#092200" label="[09:22:00]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's obvious</span></div><div class="line" id="092208"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#092208" label="[09:22:08]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Not to everyone</span></div><div class="line" id="092218"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#092218" label="[09:22:18]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ACTION shrugs</span></div><div class="line" id="092218"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#092218" label="[09:22:18]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I don't think googulator realized that</span></div><div class="line" id="092241"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#092241" label="[09:22:41]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">also, it's not constantly evolving, that's kind of the thing with the ideas I have on improving the situation</span></div><div class="line" id="092253"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#092253" label="[09:22:53]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we can/should add a metadata file to the stages (or maybe a side file, but I would like it inside?)</span></div><div class="line" id="092302"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#092302" label="[09:23:02]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tree commit used, maybe hashes of binpkgs used too</span></div><div class="line" id="092315"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#092315" label="[09:23:15]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but they start weekly on a schedule</span></div><div class="line" id="092318"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#092318" label="[09:23:18]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so it's not total chaos, is my point</span></div><div class="line" id="092342"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#092342" label="[09:23:42]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">of course, reproducing them in general is going to be harder if you want to reproduce them from scratch, and that will be a fun problem</span></div><div class="line" id="092355"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#092355" label="[09:23:55]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that might involve bringing back another stage to catalyst (which is fine)</span></div><div class="line" id="092420"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#092420" label="[09:24:20]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but i think a first good step is recording that metadata and including it either in the archive or alongside (like the sigs are now)</span></div><div class="line" id="092620"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#092620" label="[09:26:20]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'm not sure recording metadata would help a whole lot in reproducing it... It's slightly too chaotic of a process to be practical in my opinion.</span></div><div class="line" id="092634"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#092634" label="[09:26:34]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">1. provenance is useful nonetheless</span></div><div class="line" id="092642"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#092642" label="[09:26:42]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yes,</span></div><div class="line" id="092645"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#092645" label="[09:26:45]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">2. i don't agree, given the schedule i mentioned, and the possibility of tweaking that</span></div><div class="line" id="092652"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#092652" label="[09:26:52]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but we can shrug and not bother doing anything if you prefer, of course</span></div><div class="line" id="092746"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#092746" label="[09:27:46]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">🤷</span></div><div class="line" id="092849"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#092849" label="[09:28:49]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'm, in general, well-placed to talk about: a) what we do, and b) what we can change (including me supporting such changes, and maybe proposing them)</span></div><div class="line" id="092856"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#092856" label="[09:28:56]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'm not saying the status quo is perfect, but i dispute that any of it is unfixable</span></div><div class="line" id="092917"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#092917" label="[09:29:17]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's very easy to be nihilistic, it just gets us nowhere</span></div><div class="line" id="093246"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#093246" label="[09:32:46]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I have nothing to add, I agree that it's not unfixable, just commenting that the status quo is more complicated than was mentioned... (And I'm not sure if even practical to fully solve)</span></div><div class="line" id="093315"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#093315" label="[09:33:15]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">It'd require changing the way releng works</span></div><div class="line" id="094536"><span class="nick" style="color:#bc80bd"><a href="#094536" label="[09:45:36]">&lt;ztrawhcse&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: I'd also like to just add that the pandoc bootstrap issue is easily avoidable by simply doing the reasonable, sensible, sane thing and avoiding pandoc in all software ever. Don't use it, don't hook it up in package builds, scold any upstream project that adds pandoc to their `./configure &amp;&amp; make`</span></div><div class="line" id="094556"><span class="nick" style="color:#bc80bd"><a href="#094556" label="[09:45:56]">&lt;ztrawhcse&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's not even like pandoc produces good manpages, since it doesn't in fact produce good manpages</span></div><div class="line" id="115047"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#115047" label="[11:50:47]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Hello bootstrappers! A while back I mentioned my bootstrapping C compiler Onramp. I just hit a major milestone: it can finally compile Doom.</span></div><div class="line" id="115056"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#115056" label="[11:50:56]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I wrote this blog post about it: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://ludocode.com/blog/onramp-can-compile-doom">https://ludocode.com/blog/onramp-can-compile-doom</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="115905"><span class="nick" style="color:#af8d2f"><a href="#115905" label="[11:59:05]">&lt;civodul&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ludocode: amazing!!</span></div><div class="line" id="121205"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#121205" label="[12:12:05]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">a problem i see with gentoo bootstrapping with the tinycc-route i proposed: python itself is blocked indefinitely</span></div><div class="line" id="121403"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#121403" label="[12:14:03]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">this is not gentoo-specific, but it's gentoo depending on python for packaging like no other system i knew</span></div><div class="line" id="121501"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#121501" label="[12:15:01]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">an alternative portage-utility exists, from exherbo iirc, but that's written in c++ hence not applicable with the no-c++/tinycc approach either</span></div><div class="line" id="121601"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#121601" label="[12:16:01]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the original *BSD (netbsd pkgsrc and openbsd variant) tools that inspired gentoo were written in C instead</span></div><div class="line" id="121947"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#121947" label="[12:19:47]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">currently, i continued dependency-tracking and cross-compilation with gentoo-tooling on buildhost, but cannot emit a self-hosting stage-bundle anylonger, because i cannot retain python and gentoo tools for buildtarget</span></div><div class="line" id="122313"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#122313" label="[12:23:13]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and, although i got a complete system-profile for tinycc/no-c++ with gentoo, it is almost impossible to merge that back</span></div><div class="line" id="122417"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#122417" label="[12:24:17]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">of those ~500ebuilds that i kept in maintaince, almost all of them needed changes inside a hotfix overlay</span></div><div class="line" id="122458"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#122458" label="[12:24:58]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">many important ebuilds/versions vanished from the main portage tree and had to be pulled back in from gentoo archive</span></div><div class="line" id="122540"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#122540" label="[12:25:40]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">finally, i can fully fork the tree, since there's almost nothing remaining that got pulled from the main portage repository for no-c++/tinycc support</span></div><div class="line" id="122832"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#122832" label="[12:28:32]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i will however continue maintaining this giant fork, separated from and in parallel to a complete re-write of packaging to converge this with bootstrappable</span></div><div class="line" id="123340"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#123340" label="[12:33:40]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">another difference, i emitted stagle-bundles/ISOs with crossdev, instead of gentoo-catalyst tooling</span></div><div class="line" id="123405"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#123405" label="[12:34:05]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hence i had to sanitize several dozen ebuilds for crossdev</span></div><div class="line" id="123521"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#123521" label="[12:35:21]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">cdrdtools were fun ones, perl, and of cause python... it's a miracle this thing didn't fall apart yet</span></div><div class="line" id="123628"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#123628" label="[12:36:28]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">since it's various variants covered for cross-compilation, gcc, tinycc, linux-5.9, linux-2.4, musl-1.1, musl-1.2, libressl etc etc</span></div><div class="line" id="123638"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#123638" label="[12:36:38]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">full static-linking support</span></div><div class="line" id="123644"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#123644" label="[12:36:44]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">netbsd_curses</span></div><div class="line" id="123803"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#123803" label="[12:38:03]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">although i can emit a minified stage3 package set, it too contains bigger ones such as mplayer, gdb, mutt</span></div><div class="line" id="123907"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#123907" label="[12:39:07]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">currently i can crossdev target towards aarch64, aarch32, amd64, x86, while tinycc/linux24 is covers x86 &quot;only&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="123956"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#123956" label="[12:39:56]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">without qemu/binfmt btw. since cross-compilation got sanitized</span></div><div class="line" id="124825"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#124825" label="[12:48:25]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">to be precise, i can crossdev to any different ISA and keep gentoo-tooling, except for the the static/tinycc/linux24 variant, which is the most important one however</span></div><div class="line" id="125004"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#125004" label="[12:50:04]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and i think complete alternative toolchain support had belonged into gentoo, for several reasons, including bootstrapping-related ones</span></div><div class="line" id="141609"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#141609" label="[14:16:09]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ludocode: congrats on your work, hopefully it was lots of fun ^_^</span></div><div class="line" id="172015"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#172015" label="[17:20:15]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and now figured out how to get jamvm to run(wasn't obvious), next step: ant-bootstrap i think</span></div><div class="line" id="172524"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#172524" label="[17:25:24]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ludocode: is onramp available outside of github?</span></div><div class="line" id="182800"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#182800" label="[18:28:00]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: in principle you can bootstrap haskell and other languages using qemu-x86/qemu-x86_64</span></div><div class="line" id="182859"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#182859" label="[18:28:59]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: and yes, I don't have haskell on my gentoo system</span></div><div class="line" id="182932"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#182932" label="[18:29:32]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">one could even avoid ruby if you want, but it turns out bootstrappping ruby is not too bad...</span></div><div class="line" id="183634"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#183634" label="[18:36:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tmg1|michelson: can't you just reuse existing bootstrap scripts for jamvm/ant?</span></div><div class="line" id="183747"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#183747" label="[18:37:47]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you can  easily build ant with jamvm-1.5</span></div><div class="line" id="193815"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#193815" label="[19:38:15]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">what bootstrap scripts?</span></div><div class="line" id="193844"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#193844" label="[19:38:44]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'm just following <a rel="nofollow" href="https://bootstrappable.org/images/jdk-bootstrap.png">https://bootstrappable.org/images/jdk-bootstrap.png</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="202108"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#202108" label="[20:21:08]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tmg1|michelson: e.g. I have gentoo overlay here <a rel="nofollow" href="https://gitlab.com/stikonas/gentoo-bootstrap/-/blob/master/dev-java/ant-core/ant-core-1.8.4-r1.ebuild?ref_type=heads">https://gitlab.com/stikonas/gentoo-bootstrap/-/blob/master/dev-java/ant-core/ant-core-1.8.4-r1.ebuild?ref_type=heads</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="202118"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#202118" label="[20:21:18]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, this is just ant ebuild</span></div><div class="line" id="202122"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#202122" label="[20:21:22]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Oooh onramp looks like what I'd want to build to replace using tcc as a blob, nice!</span></div><div class="line" id="202303"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#202303" label="[20:23:03]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tmg1|michelson: but I have all ebuilds there from C++ to java 21</span></div><div class="line" id="202755"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#202755" label="[20:27:55]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but I guess it's easier to extract distro independent bootstrap scripting out of gentoo ebuilds than guix packages</span></div><div class="line" id="202810"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#202810" label="[20:28:10]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(since ebuilds are already in bash)</span></div><div class="line" id="205523"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#205523" label="[20:55:23]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">can't refrain, SATA and SMP both work now... when compiling/linking kernel with tinycc the order of linking objects is critical</span></div><div class="line" id="210016"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#210016" label="[21:00:16]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'm not happy with the timer subsystem yet, clock_monotonic, time64, among other things that linux-2.4 didn't have</span></div><div class="line" id="210117"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#210117" label="[21:01:17]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">with regards to kernel-bootstrapping, if tccboot loader (jit) were considered, smp remains a hindrance, since trampoline.S needs 16bit asm processing which tcc fails with</span></div><div class="line" id="210153"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#210153" label="[21:01:53]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yet absence of smp would not block kernel-bootstrap, and with an AoT compiled kernel smp does work now it seems</span></div><div class="line" id="210350"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#210350" label="[21:03:50]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">md-raid and nfs too seem affected by linking order issues that panic smpboot, it's not urgent and can be debugged on demand</span></div><div class="line" id="210846"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#210846" label="[21:08:46]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">iptables is working which failed to initialize before, ethernet bugs still with autonegotiating link-speed, i'll get to this next</span></div><div class="line" id="221202"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#221202" label="[22:12:02]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tmg1|michelson: Onramp is only on GitHub at the moment. I don't have any plans to host it elsewhere.</span></div><div class="line" id="221241"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#221241" label="[22:12:41]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lanodan: Nice, what project is that for? It doesn't quite build tcc yet but that's what I'm working on next.</span></div><div class="line" id="221413"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#221413" label="[22:14:13]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://hacktivis.me/git/bootstrap-initrd/">https://hacktivis.me/git/bootstrap-initrd/</a>  where I could consider tossing out tcc out for cproc or chibicc, tcc was just used because of being able to do `#!/bin/tcc -run`</span></div><div class="line" id="230135"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#230135" label="[23:01:35]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lanodan: Neat. The other thing about Onramp is that it doesn't produce native code. It produces bytecode for its VM. So if you wanted to produce native binaries you'd still need tcc (or another compiler), but you could include only the source for that compiler and bootstrap it with Onramp.</span></div><div class="line" id="231425"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#231425" label="[23:14:25]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I  wonder if onramp would work quicker in bridging gap between M2-Planet and tcc than mes/mescc</span></div><div class="line" id="231456"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#231456" label="[23:14:56]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ludocode: Yeah that's what I gathered, also might be mistaken but seems like core/build.sh requires utilities like echo, mkdir, … ? Which well would make sense to not need prior to onramp.</span></div><div class="line" id="231610"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#231610" label="[23:16:10]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I guess a stub version of them could be build in onramp VM bytecode, or in the case of echo as a sh.oe builtin.</span></div><div class="line" id="232013"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#232013" label="[23:20:13]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lanodan: Those are (or will be) builtin shell commands. The spec for the shell is here: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/ludocode/onramp/blob/develop/docs/shell.md">https://github.com/ludocode/onramp/blob/develop/docs/shell.md</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="232104"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#232104" label="[23:21:04]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">It's only like 20% done so far but that's the goal.</span></div><div class="line" id="232148"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#232148" label="[23:21:48]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">The shell won't depend on any external tools to run those commands. When it runs an external tool it's prefixed with &quot;onrampvm&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="232237"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#232237" label="[23:22:37]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">So when running in Onramp shell, &quot;onrampvm&quot; is a command that loads the given program into the same VM. And when running in a POSIX shell, &quot;onrampvm&quot; actually runs a new VM.</span></div><div class="line" id="232239"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#232239" label="[23:22:39]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Ooh okay, WIP</span></div><br /></div></body></html>